# Repository Guidelines

## Project Structure & Module Organization
This is a Python package laid out with a `src/`-based import path and supporting tooling.
- `src/{{project_slug_underscore}}/` contains the package code (for example `src/{{project_slug_underscore}}/main.py`).
- `tests/` holds the test suite collected by pytest.
- `pyproject.toml` and `justfile` define tooling, dependencies, and tasks.

## Build, Test, and Development Commands
Use `uv` for dependency management and to run tools. Run tasks with `just <recipe>`.
- `just install` installs dev dependencies and pre-commit hooks.
- `just sync` synchronizes dependencies.
- `just check` verifies `uv.lock` is up to date, then runs ruff (format + lint) and `ty` via pre-commit.
- `just test` runs `uv run pytest -v` and cleans `__pycache__` files.
- `just update-template` pulls in the latest changes from the copier template (requires a clean working tree).

Manage dependencies with `uv add <package>` (or `uv add --dev <package>` for tooling) rather than editing `pyproject.toml` by hand or using `pip`. Run tools with `uv run <tool>`.

## Preferred Libraries
When a task needs functionality beyond the standard library, reach for these libraries first. Only deviate when there is a concrete reason (and say why in the PR).

| Need | Use | Instead of |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Command-line interfaces | [Typer](https://typer.tiangolo.com/) | `argparse`, `click` directly |
| Logging | [loguru](https://loguru.readthedocs.io/) | the stdlib `logging` module |
| Terminal output beyond trivial `print`s (tables, progress bars, tracebacks, coloured/structured output) | [rich](https://rich.readthedocs.io/) | hand-rolled formatting, `tqdm`, `tabulate` |
| Runtime type checking / validation of object structures, and JSON/MessagePack (de)serialisation | [msgspec](https://jcristharif.com/msgspec/) (`msgspec.Struct`) | `pydantic`, `marshmallow`, manual `isinstance` checks |
| Plain data containers that don't need validation | `dataclasses` (stdlib) | ad-hoc dicts and tuples |
| Statistical data visualisation | [seaborn](https://seaborn.pydata.org/) (on top of matplotlib) | bespoke matplotlib plotting code |
| DataFrames | [polars](https://docs.pola.rs/) | `pandas` |
| Analytical SQL over local files/DataFrames | [DuckDB](https://duckdb.org/) | `sqlite3`, loading everything into memory by hand |
| HTTP clients | [httpx](https://www.python-httpx.org/) | `requests`, `urllib` |
| Retrying flaky operations | [tenacity](https://tenacity.readthedocs.io/) | hand-written retry loops |
| Filesystem paths | `pathlib` (stdlib) | `os.path` string manipulation |
| Tests | `pytest` (fixtures, `parametrize`) | `unittest`-style test classes |
| Property-based tests | [hypothesis](https://hypothesis.readthedocs.io/) | large hand-written input tables |
| Notebooks | [marimo](https://marimo.io/) | Jupyter |

## Coding Style & Naming Conventions
Follow standard Python conventions.
- Indentation is 4 spaces, no tabs.
- Use `snake_case` for functions/variables, `PascalCase` for classes, and `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for constants.
- Prefer type hints on public APIs; keep docstrings up to date.
- Run `just check` and `just test` before opening a pull request; CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yaml`) runs ruff, ty and pytest on every PR and on pushes to `main`.

## Testing Guidelines
Pytest is configured in `pyproject.toml` with coverage for `src/` and JUnit/coverage outputs.
- Place tests in `tests/` and name files `test_*.py`.
- Prefer plain pytest-style test functions; pytest will still collect existing unittest-style tests.
- Run tests with `just test`.

## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines
- Commit messages follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/); a Commitizen `commit-msg` pre-commit hook enforces this. Run `uv run cz commit` for an interactive prompt.
- Pull requests should include a concise summary, test results, and any doc updates.
- Add screenshots for documentation or UI-facing changes when relevant.

## Documentation & Configuration Notes
- Python `3.15` is required; `uv` `>=0.13.0` is expected.
